<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Visegrád</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Visegrád is a small town in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> on the bend of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. In the fourteenth century Charles I made it the royal seat, and in 1335 the kings of Hungary, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> met there to settle disputes and agree on trade. Its royal palace was rebuilt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> style under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matthias-corvinus">Matthias Corvinus</a>. The meeting gave its name to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visegrad-group">Visegrád Group</a> founded in 1991.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/visegrad.jpg" alt="Visegrád" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Szvitek Péter · CC BY 2.5 hu · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fellegv%C3%A1r_(7612._sz%C3%A1m%C3%BA_m%C5%B1eml%C3%A9k)_6.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
